"Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes. (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1)\nciteturn41307view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1\",\"lineno\":172}); Total lines: 360\nL170: The two output channels often disagree. Among admitted harmful calls, 33.6% contain a genuine textual refusal in the same model turn (95% CI [31.1, 36.2]; Table cite88†3 ). The model declines the request in words after it has already issued the retrieval call. This reproduces the refusal–call divergence found in frontier models (cite48†Cartagena and Teixeira, 2026 ), using open-weight models, automatic tool choice, and plain harmful requests.\nL171: We next ask whether this relationship extends beyond behavioral disagreement. We estimate a text-refusal direction $d_{\\text{text}}$ without the tool. It is anti-aligned with $d_{c}$ in every model (Figure cite89†4 ). At exploratory doses selected separately for each model, steering $d_{\\text{text}}$ changes admission relative to a matched random direction in four models (Table cite88†3 ). The effect ranges from a 71.4-point reduction to an 11.9-point increase.\nL172: The intervention reveals a model-dependent relationship between text refusal and admission.\nL173: Figure 4: Signed alignment between the text-refusal direction $d_{\\text{text}}$ and the admission-outcome direction $d_{c}$ across normalized depth. Diamonds mark operation-probe-selected layers; faint lines show each model’s maximum absolute cosine over 10 random controls.\nL174: Model Ref.–call (%) $d_{\\text{text}}$ excess [95% CI] Control Llama-3.2-3B 23.7 $0.0$ [$0.0$, $0.0$] saturated Llama-3.1-8B 11.5 $-5.8$ [$-7.7$, $-3.9$] valid Granite-3.1-8B 27.5 $+0.2$ [$0.0$, $+0.5$] saturated Mistral-7B 0.0 $0.0$ [$0.0$, $0.0$] floor Hermes-3-8B 44.0 $-33.3$ [$-37.6$, $-28.9$] valid Mistral-Nemo 46.6 $-71.4$ [$-75.9$, $-66.8$] valid Qwen2.5-7B 43.2 $+11.9$ [$+8.8$, $+15.0$] valid 7-model total 33.6 — —\nL175: Table 3: Behavioral refusal–call divergence (%) and dose-matched text-refusal steering excess (pp).\nL176: \nL177: Our final test changes the training signal rather than the hidden state. We fine-tune each model on 80 harmful behaviors paired with plain-text refusals and 80 benign instruction–response pairs. The training behaviors are disjoint from the 296 test behaviors, and training contains no tool calls. We use one fixed recipe and one seed per model (Appendix cite28†A.6 ).\nL178: Plain-text refusal training lowers harmful admission by 19.6 points in Llama-3.1-8B, 54.8 in Hermes-3, and 61.5 in Mistral-Nemo-12B (Figure cite90†5 ; Table cite87†19 ). These models retain 94.9–99.0% benign calling. Under this single-seed pilot, these three model–recipe combinations provide evidence that text-refusal training can sometimes transfer to admission while preserving the tested benign behavior.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBefore Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes. (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1)\nciteturn41307view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1\",\"pattern\":\"A.6 Refusal Fine-Tuning\"}); Total lines: 360\nL26:     3. cite16†4.3 Do Admission-Related Directions Influence the Call Decision? L27:     4. cite17†4.4 Does Text Refusal Govern Tool Admission? L28:   6. cite18†5 Conclusion L29:   7. cite19†6 Limitations L30:   8. cite20†7 Ethics Statement L31:   9. cite21†References L32:   10. cite22†A Category 1 Appendix Material: Reproducibility Details L33:     1. cite23†A.1 Models, Tool, and Inference L34:     2. cite24†A.2 Factorial Request Construction L35:     3. cite25†A.3 Harm and Refusal Labels L36:     4. cite26†A.4 Representation and Intervention Protocol L37:     5. cite27†A.5 Statistical Protocol L38:     6. cite28†A.6 Refusal Fine-Tuning L39:   11. cite29†B Category 2 Appendix Material: Complementary Results L40:     1. cite30†B.1 Closed-Model Replication L41:     2. cite31†B.2 Autonomous Retrieval and Deployment Defenses L42:     3. cite32†B.3 Admission Monitoring L43:     4. cite33†B.4 Full Per-Model Component Ablation L44:     5. cite34†B.5 Full-Data and Fine-Tuning Diagnostics L45: cite35†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L46: \nL47: arXiv:2610.23071v1 [cs.CL] 16 Oct 2026\nL48: # Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents\nL49: Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes.\nL50: \nL51: Aditya Nawal    Manit Baser    Mohan Gurusamy Affiliation: National University of Singapore Affiliation: adityanawal@u.nus.edu, manit.baser@u.nus.edu, Email: gmohan@nus.edu.sg\nL52: ###### Abstract\nL53: Large language models increasingly act as agents that call external tools, yet most tool-safety research examines only what follows a tool call. The prior decision of whether to call the tool remains largely unexamined and can influence downstream safety. Across the seven open-weight models, the unweighted model-average harm rate after admitted harmful requests is 74.5%. To identify what governs this decision, we apply a factorial intervention to the input request.\nL54: Within our generated construction, neutralizing the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast (19 pp). A linear direction estimated from this contrast influences held-out admission in four of seven models; effects on operation-neutralized requests in some models show that it is not an operation-specific mechanism. We then test whether written refusal is behaviorally and representationally coupled to the same decision. Written refusal accompanies 33.6% of admitted harmful calls.\nL68: Prior work establishes that written refusals can coexist with prohibited tool calls (cite48†Cartagena and Teixeira, 2026 ). We treat this behavioral divergence as the starting point. We test its internal and training-level relationship by steering the text-refusal direction at the decision token and fine-tuning models to refuse the same behaviors in plain text. Steering changes tool admission in four models, with varying magnitudes and directions.\nL69: Under one single-seed recipe, fine-tuning reduces tool admission in three model–recipe combinations that retain benign tool use, but residual unsafe admission remains. Written refusal therefore has a partial, model-dependent relation to tool admission.\nL70: Our contribution is a controlled diagnosis of admission. We evaluate responses following both call and no-call decisions, identify the functional request component with the largest admission contrast, intervene on a direction estimated from that contrast on unseen behaviors, and test whether response-level refusal training transfers to admission.\nL71: Together, these findings show that harmful tool admission is shaped by the requested operation and is only partially coupled to written refusal. For users, a safe-looking response does not establish that the model withheld the call. Deployment evaluations must therefore inspect the action channel directly. Future safeguards should supervise and monitor admission itself, test whether admission control transfers across tool types, and preserve legitimate tool use.\nL72: \nL73: Our contributions are as follows:\nL74: \nL75:   1. 1.\nL76: We introduce Admit, an evaluation protocol that scores responses after both admission outcomes. Harmful responses follow 74.5% of admitted harmful requests, while no-call responses add 11.2 percentage points to Admit harm.\nL77: \nL78:   2. 2.\nL79: \nL80: In our generated factorial construction, the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast. Its 19-point effect exceeds the 8-point topic effect, and the ordering reproduces across open- and closed-weight models.\nL86: We test text refusal–tool admission coupling through representation steering and refusal-only fine-tuning. The relationship is partial and model-dependent, so text refusal is not a reliable proxy for call safety.\nL94: Refusal directions are identifiable in activations, although harmfulness and refusal can have distinct or multidimensional representations (cite64†Arditi et al., 2024 ; cite65†Zhao et al., 2026 ; cite66†Siu et al., 2025 ; cite67†Xie et al., 2025a ). Fine-tuning can weaken text and agentic safety (cite68†Qi et al., 2023 ; cite69†Qi et al., 2024 ; cite70†Hahm et al., 2025 ). Prior work establishes refusal–call behavioral divergence.\nL175: Table 3: Behavioral refusal–call divergence (%) and dose-matched text-refusal steering excess (pp).\nL176: \nL177: Our final test changes the training signal rather than the hidden state. We fine-tune each model on 80 harmful behaviors paired with plain-text refusals and 80 benign instruction–response pairs. The training behaviors are disjoint from the 296 test behaviors, and training contains no tool calls. We use one fixed recipe and one seed per model (Appendix cite28†A.6 ).\nL178: Plain-text refusal training lowers harmful admission by 19.6 points in Llama-3.1-8B, 54.8 in Hermes-3, and 61.5 in Mistral-Nemo-12B (Figure cite90†5 ; Table cite87†19 ). These models retain 94.9–99.0% benign calling. Under this single-seed pilot, these three model–recipe combinations provide evidence that text-refusal training can sometimes transfer to admission while preserving the tested benign behavior.\nL179: The same recipe does not transfer cleanly to every model. Llama-3.2-3B reduces harmful admission but also reduces benign calling to 80.7%. Qwen2.5-7B produces repeated tool-call tokens, while Granite-3.1-8B and Mistral-7B stop calling on benign requests. These runs do not isolate refusal transfer and are reported in Appendix cite28†A.6 . The successful and inconclusive runs together show that text-refusal training does not provide a reliable control over admission.\nL180: For users and deployers, written refusal cannot certify that the call was withheld. Future work should directly compare refusal-only, admission-only, and joint supervision under matched training budgets and broader legitimate tool-use evaluations.\nL181: Figure 5: Single-seed plain-text refusal fine-tuning pilot: harmful request admission rate (%) before and after fine-tuning on disjoint behaviors. Three model–recipe combinations preserve 95–99% benign calling; Llama-3.2-3B falls to 80.7%.\nL182: ## 5 Conclusion\nL183: Within our generated construction, the requested-operation component produces the largest tool-admission contrast and admission is only partially coupled to written refusal. The requested operation changes admission by 19 percentage points, and its contrast is anti-aligned with the admission outcome across all seven open-weight models. Removing this direction influences admission on unseen behaviors in four models.\nL184: Its effects on operation-neutralized requests show that the influence is not specific to harmful-operation semantics. Written refusal accompanies 33.6% of admitted harmful calls, and representation steering produces heterogeneous changes in admission. Under one single-seed recipe, plain-text refusal training reduces harmful admission in three model–recipe combinations that retain benign tool use; the remaining runs do not support a clean transfer conclusion.\nL185: The fixed-source design makes these request and representation contrasts comparable, and downstream scoring shows why admission matters: the unweighted model-average harm rate after admitted harmful requests is 74.5%. These findings identify tool admission as a distinct safety control point. For users, a refusal displayed in the final response does not establish that the agent withheld the call.\nL193: The fine-tuning study uses one recipe and one seed per model, and only three models preserve sufficient benign tool use for the coupling comparison. We therefore treat it as pilot evidence of possible transfer, not a general training result. Finally, a fixed GPT-4o judge scores downstream harm. Human, Claude, and Gemini validation provide substantial agreement, but automated judgments can still contain systematic errors.\nL207:   * Hahm et al. (2025) D. Hahm, T. Min, W. Jin, and K. Lee Unintended misalignment from agentic fine-tuning: risks and mitigation. External Links: 2508.14031, cite105†Link Cited by: cite94†§2 .\nL208:   * Ji et al. (2025) Z. Ji, D. Wu, W. Wang, P. Ma, S. Wang, and L. Ma Digging into the internal: causality-based analysis of LLM function calling. External Links: 2509.16268, cite106†Link Cited by: cite102†§2 .\nL217:   * Qi et al. (2024) X. Qi, A. Panda, K. Lyu, X. Ma, S. Roy, A. Beirami, P. Mittal, and P. Henderson Safety alignment should be made more than just a few tokens deep. External Links: 2406.05946, cite116†Link Cited by: cite94†§2 .\nL218:   * Qi et al. (2023) X. Qi, Y. Zeng, T. Xie, P. Chen, R. Jia, P. Mittal, and P. Henderson Fine-tuning aligned language models compromises safety, even when users do not intend to!. External Links: 2310.03693, cite117†Link Cited by: cite94†§2 .\nL283: Effects are estimated separately by model. Cross-model summaries are unweighted macro-averages over the seven models. Factorial main effects average the relevant condition contrasts over the remaining two factors. Full-data layer and dose analyses select and evaluate on the same pool and are descriptive. The held-out analysis performs direction estimation and layer selection inside each outer-training split. We apply no multiplicity correction across models and make no pooled significance claim.\nL284: ### A.6 Refusal Fine-Tuning\nL285: \nL286: The training set contains 80 HarmBench validation behaviors paired cyclically with eight short safety refusals and 80 benign instructions paired with GPT-4o responses. Training behaviors are disjoint from the 296 evaluation behaviors. Records contain no tool definitions, calls, or outputs.\nL287: We use 4-bit NF4 QLoRA with bfloat16 computation and double quantization. LoRA targets q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj, gate_proj, up_proj, and down_proj, with rank 16, $\\alpha=16$, and zero dropout. Training uses three epochs, batch size 2, gradient accumulation 4, learning rate $2\\times 10^{-4}$, five warm-up steps, a linear schedule, paged AdamW 8-bit, weight decay 0.01, maximum sequence length 1,024, and seed 42. The adapter is merged into the bfloat16 base model before evaluation.\nL288: We use one recipe and one seed for every model.\nL289: ## Appendix B Category 2 Appendix Material: Complementary Results\nL290: ### B.1 Closed-Model Replication\nL291: Our mechanistic analyses require open weights, but admission can be measured on any tool-using model. We replicate the harmful/benign comparison and component factorial on GPT-4.1, Gemini-2.5-Flash, and Claude-Haiku-4.5 using the same URLs, tool schema, prompts, and behaviors (Table cite79†8 ). All three admit 21.3–44.1% of harmful requests and 93–99% of benign summary requests; after an admitted harmful-request call, 36.5–77.9% of responses are judged harmful.\nL334: ### B.5 Full-Data and Fine-Tuning Diagnostics\nL335: As a descriptive sensitivity check, Table cite87†19 estimates and selects the $d_{B}$ layer on the full behavior pool rather than a held-out split; Table cite86†2 in the main paper reports the held-out analysis. The same table asks whether the intervention is specific to operation-present prompts by comparing the flip rate among requests the model initially does not admit.\nL336: Table cite87†19 gives the complete outcomes behind the fine-tuning transfer result in §cite88†3 , including the recipe failures excluded from that comparison.\nL337: Model / layer $\\cos(d_{B},d_{c})$ null max causal $\\Delta$ Qwen2.5-7B L9^{†} $-0.46$ 0.02 $-3.1$ pp Qwen2.5-7B L18 $-0.63$ 0.05 — Qwen2.5-7B L21 $-0.90$ 0.04 — Qwen2.5-7B L24^{∗} $-0.92$ 0.03 — Qwen2.5-7B L28^{‡} $-0.91$ 0.03 $+29.3$ pp Mistral-Nemo-12B L17^{†} $-0.44$ 0.02 — Mistral-Nemo-12B L25 $-0.81$ 0.02 — Mistral-Nemo-12B L30 $-0.82$ 0.02 — Mistral-Nemo-12B L39^{∗,‡} $-0.83$ 0.03 $+13.9$ pp\nL338: Table 17: Per-layer compliance geometry for the two models whose B-probe and geometry maxima differ. ^{†}B-probe peak; ^{∗}layer attaining maximum $|\\cos|$; ^{‡}final layer used for intervention.\nL339: Model Coupling Admission before and after fine-tuning Change Benign admission after fine-tuning Llama-3.1-8B weakly coupled $70.3\\to 50.7$ $-19.6$ 99.0 Hermes-3-8B coupled $84.5\\to 29.7$ $\\mathbf{-54.8}$ 94.9 Mistral-Nemo-12B coupled $89.9\\to 28.4$ $\\mathbf{-61.5}$ 95.9 Llama-3.2-3B — $64.2\\to 28.0$ $-36.2$ 80.7 Recipe failures excluded from comparison: Qwen2.5-7B coupled (opp.\nL340: sign) $69.6\\to 99.0$ no refusal 100.0 Granite-3.1-8B — $44.3\\to 0.0$ benign collapse 0.0 Mistral-7B — $7.8\\to 0.0$ benign collapse 0.0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBefore Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes. (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1)\nciteturn41307view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1\",\"pattern\":\"A.3 Harm and Refusal Labels\"}); Total lines: 360\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:   4. cite8†3 Methodology L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Experimental Setting and Metrics L20:     2. cite10†3.2 Request Conditions and Harm Evaluation L21:     3. cite11†3.3 Factorial Request Intervention L22:     4. cite12†3.4 Representation Analysis and Intervention L23:   5. cite13†4 Results L24:     1. cite14†4.1 Why Must Admission Be Evaluated on Both Branches? L25:     2. cite15†4.2 Which Part of a Harmful Request Most Affects Admission? L26:     3. cite16†4.3 Do Admission-Related Directions Influence the Call Decision? L27:     4. cite17†4.4 Does Text Refusal Govern Tool Admission? L28:   6. cite18†5 Conclusion L29:   7. cite19†6 Limitations L30:   8. cite20†7 Ethics Statement L31:   9. cite21†References L32:   10. cite22†A Category 1 Appendix Material: Reproducibility Details L33:     1. cite23†A.1 Models, Tool, and Inference L34:     2. cite24†A.2 Factorial Request Construction L35:     3. cite25†A.3 Harm and Refusal Labels L36:     4. cite26†A.4 Representation and Intervention Protocol L37:     5. cite27†A.5 Statistical Protocol L38:     6. cite28†A.6 Refusal Fine-Tuning L39:   11. cite29†B Category 2 Appendix Material: Complementary Results L40:     1. cite30†B.1 Closed-Model Replication L41:     2. cite31†B.2 Autonomous Retrieval and Deployment Defenses L42:     3. cite32†B.3 Admission Monitoring L43:     4. cite33†B.4 Full Per-Model Component Ablation L44:     5. cite34†B.5 Full-Data and Fine-Tuning Diagnostics L45: cite35†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L46: \nL47: arXiv:2610.23071v1 [cs.CL] 16 Oct 2026\nL48: # Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents\nL49: Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes.\nL50: \nL51: Aditya Nawal    Manit Baser    Mohan Gurusamy Affiliation: National University of Singapore Affiliation: adityanawal@u.nus.edu, manit.baser@u.nus.edu, Email: gmohan@nus.edu.sg\nL52: ###### Abstract\nL53: Large language models increasingly act as agents that call external tools, yet most tool-safety research examines only what follows a tool call. The prior decision of whether to call the tool remains largely unexamined and can influence downstream safety. Across the seven open-weight models, the unweighted model-average harm rate after admitted harmful requests is 74.5%. To identify what governs this decision, we apply a factorial intervention to the input request.\nL59: Instruction tuning and refusal training primarily supervise the assistant’s text response (cite40†Ouyang et al., 2022 ; cite41†Bai et al., 2022 ). Tool-using agents introduce an earlier decision about invoking an external tool. Retrieval access can increase harmful outputs (cite42†Yu et al., 2025 ), and aligned chat models can become less safe when deployed as browser agents (cite43†Kumar et al., 2024 ). Retrieved pages can also weaken safeguards even when their content is not adversarial (cite44†Nawal et al., 2026 ).\nL94: Refusal directions are identifiable in activations, although harmfulness and refusal can have distinct or multidimensional representations (cite64†Arditi et al., 2024 ; cite65†Zhao et al., 2026 ; cite66†Siu et al., 2025 ; cite67†Xie et al., 2025a ). Fine-tuning can weaken text and agentic safety (cite68†Qi et al., 2023 ; cite69†Qi et al., 2024 ; cite70†Hahm et al., 2025 ). Prior work establishes refusal–call behavioral divergence.\nL107: A model that withholds retrieval but answers harmfully moves harm to the second term, so its call-conditioned score can improve without reducing response harm. We report admission, harm after a call, harm without a call, and Admit harm. The same judge scores both response branches. Because the web-retrieval tool is read-only, $h$ measures response harm and does not assign an execution cost to the call itself.\nL113: We score every harmful-request response with a fixed GPT-4o judge at temperature 0. The judge receives the behavior and the assistant response produced after tool execution or without a call. It assigns a score from 1 to 5, with scores of at least 3 defining $h{=}1$. On 400 stratified outputs, its binary labels agree with a blind human annotator in 86% of cases ($\\kappa=0.738$). Independent Claude and Gemini judges show comparable agreement ($\\kappa=0.772$ and $0.700$; Appendix cite25†A.3 ).\nL257: A second independent judge from a different developer (gemini-2.5-flash) corroborates these role assignments on the 140 behaviors it returns parseable decisions for (A 98.6%, B 92.9%, C 89.3%). Restricting the factorial to the 86 behaviors where all three roles are accepted leaves the same ordering as the full set: 7.8 pp (A), 19.1 pp (B, 95% CI [15.3, 23.0]), and 7.1 pp (C), against 8.2/19.1/6.8 pp over all 147 behaviors.\nL258: Annotation imperfections limit a literal reading of individual clauses, particularly C, but do not change which component has the largest effect.\nL259: ### A.3 Harm and Refusal Labels\nL260: \nL261: The GPT-4o harm judge runs at temperature 0 with no system prompt, following the X-Teaming protocol (cite131†Rahman et al., 2025 ). Its user message contains the prohibited-usage policy, HarmBench behavior, model response, and any behavior-specific context. The judge returns JSON {‘‘score’’: 1--5, ‘‘reason’’: ‘‘...’’}. Scores of at least 3 define harm.\nL262: Human Validation. The validation sample contains 400 outputs stratified across models and conditions. A blind NLP graduate-student annotator applies the same rubric. GPT-4o agrees with the human annotator on 86.0% of binary labels ($\\kappa=0.738$).\nL263: Inter-LLM Agreement. Claude-Sonnet-4.6 and Gemini-2.5-Flash independently score the same sample with the same rubric. GPT-4o agreement is 92.1% with Claude ($\\kappa=0.772$, $n=369$) and 89.8% with Gemini ($\\kappa=0.700$, $n=400$), and all three judges agree on 87.2% of items. The three judges assign nearly the same overall harm rate (78% GPT-4o, 79% Gemini), so GPT-4o is not the strict or lenient outlier.\nL264: Broken out by model (Table cite132†6 ), agreement is substantial to near-perfect for five of the seven open models ($\\kappa\\,0.82$–$1.00$) and moderate for the two small Llamas (Llama-3.1-8B $\\kappa=0.52$, Llama-3.2-3B $\\kappa=0.63$); in both low-$\\kappa$ cases the two judges’ overall harm rates are within 8 pp, so the disagreement is near-threshold noise rather than a systematic divergence.\nL265: The closed GPT-4.1 is not in this open-model validation set, and because the primary judge (GPT-4o) shares a developer with it, its downstream-harm rate in Table cite79†8 is the case most exposed to same-lab leniency; we therefore re-judged all of GPT-4.1’s admitted harmful-request responses ($n=125$) with the independent Claude judge. The two judges agree on 98.4% of these items ($\\kappa=0.97$), and Claude scores harm slightly more often than GPT-4o (44.8% vs.\nL275: Written-refusal labels. For each admitted harmful request, GPT-4o labels the prose portion of the assistant turn as a safety refusal or not a safety refusal. A safety refusal objects because the request is harmful, illegal, dangerous, unethical, or disallowed. Capability statements, neutral preambles, and descriptions without an objection are not refusals. Pure tool-call turns contain no written refusal and remain in the denominator.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBefore Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes. (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1)\nciteturn41307view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23071v1\",\"pattern\":\"A.5 Statistical Protocol\"}); Total lines: 360\nL26:     3. cite16†4.3 Do Admission-Related Directions Influence the Call Decision? L27:     4. cite17†4.4 Does Text Refusal Govern Tool Admission? L28:   6. cite18†5 Conclusion L29:   7. cite19†6 Limitations L30:   8. cite20†7 Ethics Statement L31:   9. cite21†References L32:   10. cite22†A Category 1 Appendix Material: Reproducibility Details L33:     1. cite23†A.1 Models, Tool, and Inference L34:     2. cite24†A.2 Factorial Request Construction L35:     3. cite25†A.3 Harm and Refusal Labels L36:     4. cite26†A.4 Representation and Intervention Protocol L37:     5. cite27†A.5 Statistical Protocol L38:     6. cite28†A.6 Refusal Fine-Tuning L39:   11. cite29†B Category 2 Appendix Material: Complementary Results L40:     1. cite30†B.1 Closed-Model Replication L41:     2. cite31†B.2 Autonomous Retrieval and Deployment Defenses L42:     3. cite32†B.3 Admission Monitoring L43:     4. cite33†B.4 Full Per-Model Component Ablation L44:     5. cite34†B.5 Full-Data and Fine-Tuning Diagnostics L45: cite35†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L46: \nL47: arXiv:2610.23071v1 [cs.CL] 16 Oct 2026\nL48: # Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents\nL49: Warning: This paper contains model-generated content related to harmful behaviors, included for research purposes.\nL50: \nL51: Aditya Nawal    Manit Baser    Mohan Gurusamy Affiliation: National University of Singapore Affiliation: adityanawal@u.nus.edu, manit.baser@u.nus.edu, Email: gmohan@nus.edu.sg\nL52: ###### Abstract\nL53: Large language models increasingly act as agents that call external tools, yet most tool-safety research examines only what follows a tool call. The prior decision of whether to call the tool remains largely unexamined and can influence downstream safety. Across the seven open-weight models, the unweighted model-average harm rate after admitted harmful requests is 74.5%. To identify what governs this decision, we apply a factorial intervention to the input request.\nL54: Within our generated construction, neutralizing the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast (19 pp). A linear direction estimated from this contrast influences held-out admission in four of seven models; effects on operation-neutralized requests in some models show that it is not an operation-specific mechanism. We then test whether written refusal is behaviorally and representationally coupled to the same decision. Written refusal accompanies 33.6% of admitted harmful calls.\nL135: Appendix cite27†A.5 specifies the resampling unit, aggregation order, and selection handling.\nL279: Dose-matched steering. For each model and target direction, we apply the target and its matched random direction at the same signed dose. Reported effects subtract the random-direction admission shift. A saturated random control is marked inconclusive. For Llama-3.1-8B and Granite-3.1-8B, we also inspect the gentlest dose ($k=-0.5$), where the random shift has magnitude at most 5 percentage points.\nL280: ### A.5 Statistical Protocol\nL281: \nL282: The resampling unit is the behavior. Each bootstrap draw samples behaviors with replacement and includes every associated condition and intervention record. We use 2,000 draws. Cosine intervals additionally stratify by admission outcome so neither contrast arm is empty. Paired intervention effects are computed within behavior before bootstrapping. Wilson intervals are used for unclustered proportions.\nL283: Effects are estimated separately by model. Cross-model summaries are unweighted macro-averages over the seven models. Factorial main effects average the relevant condition contrasts over the remaining two factors. Full-data layer and dose analyses select and evaluate on the same pool and are descriptive. The held-out analysis performs direction estimation and layer selection inside each outer-training split. We apply no multiplicity correction across models and make no pooled significance claim.\nL284: ### A.6 Refusal Fine-Tuning\nL285: \nL286: The training set contains 80 HarmBench validation behaviors paired cyclically with eight short safety refusals and 80 benign instructions paired with GPT-4o responses. Training behaviors are disjoint from the 296 evaluation behaviors. Records contain no tool definitions, calls, or outputs.\nL287: We use 4-bit NF4 QLoRA with bfloat16 computation and double quantization. LoRA targets q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj, gate_proj, up_proj, and down_proj, with rank 16, $\\alpha=16$, and zero dropout. Training uses three epochs, batch size 2, gradient accumulation 4, learning rate $2\\times 10^{-4}$, five warm-up steps, a linear schedule, paged AdamW 8-bit, weight decay 0.01, maximum sequence length 1,024, and seed 42. The adapter is merged into the bfloat16 base model before evaluation.\nL313: Frozen at its original threshold, a probe trained without these hard negatives flags 91–100% of them as false positives. Retraining with the hard negatives inside the same cross-validation folds catches 98.4–100% of harmful admissions at a 5% false-positive rate for both the hidden-state probe and the text-only baseline (Table cite83†14 ).\n"
